This project has been tested using Arduino IDE 1.8.19 with MightyCore v2.2.2, so verified instructions can only be provided (by me) for this environment.
However, the project can still be compiled and programmed into the MCU using Arduino IDE 2.x.

Why MightyCore 2.2.2?
Older versions lack critical functionalities required for this project (such as the ability to configure the serial buffer size).
Newer versions (starting from v3.x) replaced the classic Optiboot bootloader with a new one called Urboot.
Urboot requires AVRDUDE 7.x or higher to handle serial uploads, whereas Arduino IDE 1.8.19 uses AVRDUDE 6.3.

Furthermore, two files from original 2.2.2 MightyCore need to be replaced:

1)
Even though MightyCore 2.2.2 is the correct core to use for IDE 1.8.19, its bundled avrdude.conf file was already prepared for the transition to AVRDUDE 7.x.
It contains modern directives—such as default_spi = "";—that the older AVRDUDE 6.3 interpreter cannot understand.
If you attempt to burn the bootloader or upload sketches using MightyCore's stock configuration file, AVRDUDE 6.3 will abort with a fatal syntax error.

The Required FixTo solve this syntax conflict and ensure flawless operation, you must replace the configuration file bundled with MightyCore with the native one from your Arduino IDE installation:

Source File: arduino-1.8.19/hardware/tools/avr/etc/avrdude.conf
Destination Path: hardware/MightyCore/avr/avrdude.conf

By overwriting MightyCore's configuration file with the IDE's internal version, you perfectly align the syntax with AVRDUDE 6.3, eliminating all syntax errors during the flashing process.

2)
A modified boards.txt file is included in this repository.
Use it to overwrite the stock MightyCore file located at hardware/MightyCore/avr/boards.txt.

This modified version applies two enhancements:

Lock Byte Alignment (0x0F instead of 0xCF):
It ensures compatibility with the AVRDUDE version bundled with Arduino IDE 1.8.19.
Newer configurations and internal bitmasks in avrdude.conf expect the unused bits (D7:D6) to be handled as zeros logistically during the verification process.
Using the older 0xCF value would cause a validation error.

Serial Buffer Sizes: It configures larger (1KB) serial buffer sizes, useful for the Z80-MBC2 IOS firmware to better handle data transfers over the serial port.




CANCELLAZIONE DEL MICRO:
arduino-1.8.19/hardware/tools/avr/bin/avrdude -Carduino-1.8.19/hardware/MightyCore/avr/avrdude.conf -v -patmega1284p -cstk500v1 -P/dev/ttyACM0 -b115200 -e


PROGRAMMAZIONE FILE .HEX + fuses configurati per non usare il bootloader:

stk500v1:
arduino-1.8.19/hardware/tools/avr/bin/avrdude -Carduino-1.8.19/hardware/MightyCore/avr/avrdude.conf -v -patmega1284p -cstk500v1 -P/dev/ttyACM0 -b115200 -e -Ulfuse:w:0xF7:m -Uhfuse:w:0xD7:m -Uefuse:w:0xFD:m -Ulock:w:0x3F:m -Uflash:w:firmware.hex:i

PROGRAMMAZIONE FILE .HEX + fuses configurati per firmware+bootloader:

arduino-1.8.19/hardware/tools/avr/bin/avrdude -Carduino-1.8.19/hardware/MightyCore/avr/avrdude.conf -v -patmega1284p -cstk500v1 -P/dev/ttyACM0 -b115200 -e -Ulfuse:w:0xF7:m -Uhfuse:w:0xD6:m -Uefuse:w:0xFD:m -Ulock:w:0x3F:m -Uflash:w:firmware.with_bootloader.hex:i -Ulock:w:0x0F:m

